      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Solve: $y = 3x - 1$ and $y = x^2 + x - 1$.",
      "options": [
        { "id": "a", "text": "$(0, -1)$ and $(2, 5)$" },
        { "id": "b", "text": "$(1, 2)$ and $(-2, -7)$" },
        { "id": "c", "text": "$(0, -1)$ and $(-2, -7)$" },
        { "id": "d", "text": "$(2, 5)$ and $(1, 2)$" }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "a",
      "explanation": "Set equal: $3x - 1 = x^2 + x - 1$. Simplify: $x^2 - 2x = 0$. Factorise: $x(x - 2) = 0$. So $x = 0, y = -1$ or $x = 2, y = 5$."
